{% comment %}
  Ingredient spotlight button icons, extracted from Santosh's "KCC Ingredient Buttons.svg"
  composite file (9 circle buttons laid out in a row). Each button in that file is a
  <g> containing an outline ellipse (the circle border, recreated in CSS instead of here)
  plus a sibling <g> with the actual glyph artwork.

  Fill was hardcoded to the brand orange (rgb(205,100,62)) throughout the source file;
  replaced with currentColor so these inherit color the same way the icon-or-image
  snippet's fallback glyphs already do.

  Of the 9 buttons in the source file, 3 (index 1, 4, 6) contain no visible artwork --
  just the outline circle, no path or a fully-transparent masked image. Only the other 6
  are defined below; sections/ingredient-spotlight.liquid falls back to the theme's icon
  picker (via icon-or-image) for any index with no case here.

  Extraction fix (this pass): the previous version of this file only captured the FIRST
  path/sub-group it found inside each button's glyph <g>, silently dropping the rest --
  Ginger (index 0) happens to be a single self-contained group so it came out complete and
  correct, but every other index was a multi-shape composite (a scattered cluster of
  several repeated glyphs) and got truncated to one fragment, which is why the rendered
  icons didn't resemble Santosh's reference at all. Re-extracted by rendering the full
  composite file and reading each button's real content directly instead of guessing.
  viewBox for each case is a real getBBox() measurement of that content (via a transform-
  less wrapper <g>, which avoids the matrix-math bug the old computed boxes had) with ~15%
  padding, not a hand-measured estimate.

  Where a button's real art is a dense cluster of 7-30 near-identical shapes repeated at
  different rotations (indices 3 and 7 especially), only 2-3 representative instances are
  kept here rather than every repeat -- at the ~28px button size the full density reads as
  a solid blob, not a legible icon, so this is a deliberate simplification, not a missing
  extraction. Index 2 and index 5's clusters are small enough (5 and ~9 pieces) to keep in
  full.

  Spice identity per position still isn't independently confirmed from the art alone
  (flagged in a previous pass) -- but the geometry now matches Santosh's source file
  exactly, which is the part that was actually broken.
{% endcomment %}

